Build a polished mobile-first prototype for a calm, premium puzzle game where the player restores a fractured Skybox AI 3D scene by dragging irregular tiles into place.

This should feel elegant, tactile, minimal, and satisfying. Do not make it feel like a cheap jigsaw game. The visual mood should be calm and premium.

CORE CONCEPT
- The source image is a Skybox AI-generated 3D scene, typically a panoramic or immersive-looking landscape image with strong depth and atmosphere.
- At the start of the prototype, prompt the user to upload an image so they can test the system using their own Skybox AI scene.
- After upload, display that uploaded scene as though it has been fractured into large, irregular Voronoi-style tiles.
- One tile begins already correctly placed and acts as the anchor.
- A small central playable area is initially available.
- The rest of the scene is hidden behind a soft atmospheric fog / veil.
- The player drags loose tiles into their correct places.
- When a tile is placed correctly, it snaps in satisfyingly.
- Loose tiles should appear desaturated / grayscale / muted.
- When a tile is correctly placed, full colour returns to that tile.
- When the initial area is completed, the fog recedes to reveal another connected area of the scene.
- The scene should gradually be restored in elegant stages.

IMPORTANT DESIGN GOALS
- Must feel calm and premium, not busy or arcade-like.
- Minimal UI.
- No loud effects, no intrusive scoring, no clutter.
- Focus on tactile drag feel, snap feel, visual clarity, and graceful transitions.
- Think “fractured world being restored”, not “mobile jigsaw”.

PLATFORM / FORMAT
- Mobile portrait layout.
- Single-page HTML/CSS/JS prototype.
- Touch-first controls, but mouse should also work on desktop.
- No frameworks required unless absolutely necessary. Plain JS preferred for portability.
- Use canvas if that is the cleanest way to render and manipulate the scene and puzzle pieces.

UPLOAD FLOW
- On first load, show an elegant, minimal upload screen asking the user to upload a Skybox AI scene image for demo purposes.
- This should feel on-brand with the calm premium tone, not like a default browser file input dumped on screen.
- After image upload, initialize the puzzle using the uploaded image.
- Include a fallback demo image option as well, in case the user wants to test without uploading.

VISUAL STYLE
- The uploaded scene should be treated as a dramatic 3D-looking world image with depth, atmosphere, horizon, clouds, landforms, etc.
- The scene should be divided into irregular but readable Voronoi-like cells.
- Tile boundaries should be subtle and elegant, not bright debug-looking overlays.
- Use thin, soft, low-contrast seam lines.
- Overall presentation should feel restrained and tasteful.

PUZZLE STRUCTURE
- Use a modest number of large irregular tiles, enough to prove the concept without becoming messy.
- Start with a small central zone only, roughly equivalent to an easy 4x4-area puzzle section in complexity.
- One tile inside this zone should already be locked in place and shown in full colour.
- The other tiles for that zone should be staged around the edges of the playable area in a neat, calm layout.
- Do not scatter pieces randomly all over the screen in a chaotic way.
- After completing the first zone, reveal a second connected zone by receding the fog.
- It is enough for this prototype to have 2 reveal stages.

FOG / VEIL MECHANIC
- The unrevealed parts of the image should be hidden behind a soft fog / veil / haze rather than a hard black mask.
- The fog should feel atmospheric and premium.
- When a zone is completed, the fog should retreat smoothly and elegantly.
- The reveal should preserve a sense of discovery and reduce visual overwhelm.

COLOUR RESTORATION MECHANIC
- Loose movable tiles should appear in a muted grayscale or desaturated state.
- Correctly placed tiles should regain full colour on snap.
- The already-anchored starting tile should begin in full colour.
- This mechanic is important and should be central to the feel of restoration.

SNAP / PLACEMENT FEEL
- Correct placement should feel excellent.
- Add a gentle magnetic attraction when a tile is near the correct location.
- Snap threshold should be forgiving enough to feel smooth on mobile.
- When a piece snaps in:
  - animate a subtle settle into place
  - restore full colour
  - optionally add a soft glow or brief polish effect
- Incorrect placement should simply leave the piece movable, without harsh punishment.

ROTATION
- Include rotation as an optional mechanic in the prototype, but keep it elegant and restrained.
- Do NOT use fiddly free rotation.
- Use 90-degree step rotation only.
- Best interaction: when a loose tile is selected, show a subtle rotate button/control nearby.
- For this prototype, only some tiles in the second revealed zone need rotation.
- The first zone should be solvable without rotation so the opening feels calm and approachable.

READABILITY / USABILITY
- Irregular pieces must remain large and readable on a phone screen.
- Avoid tiny fragments.
- Prioritize landmark readability and clean interaction.
- Keep the number of active loose tiles manageable.
- The prototype should feel easy to understand and pleasant to interact with.

SCENE / TILE QUALITY LOGIC
- If possible, include simple logic to avoid reveal zones made mostly of bland, similar tiles.
- For example, do not let the first zone be mostly empty sky or low-detail gradients.
- A simple heuristic is enough:
  - prefer tiles with stronger contrast / edges / landmarks
  - ensure each revealed zone has some visual variety
- This does not need to be perfect, but should be considered in the structure.

SETTINGS / CONTROL PANEL
- Add a subtle settings icon in the interface.
- Tapping it should open a discreet control panel for tweaking important gameplay and presentation variables.
- This is mainly for developer/demo tuning, so it does not need to feel player-facing or gamey, but it should still look clean and restrained.
- Suggested tweakable controls:
  - number of tiles / partition density
  - snap strength
  - snap distance threshold
  - fog opacity
  - reveal zone size
  - number of reveal stages
  - amount of desaturation on loose tiles
  - whether rotation is enabled
  - how many tiles in stage 2 require rotation
  - seam visibility
  - tile staging distance from board
  - optional debug outlines / labels toggle
- The settings panel should be hidden by default behind the subtle icon.
- Changes should update the prototype live where practical, or rebuild the puzzle when needed.

UI
- Keep UI extremely minimal.
- Include:
  - subtle settings icon
  - small reset button if useful
  - elegant upload prompt on first load
- Small debug toggle is fine if useful, but hidden inside settings.
- No score counters, timers, stars, currency, or arcade-style overlays.
- A tiny stage indicator is acceptable if tasteful.

AUDIO
- Optional only if quick to implement.
- If included, keep it very subtle:
  - soft click / ceramic / glassy settle sound on correct placement
- No loud arcade sounds.

ANIMATION / POLISH
- Use gentle easing.
- Tiles should lift slightly or feel subtly elevated when dragged.
- Snapping should feel soft but confident.
- Fog reveal should feel smooth and deliberate.
- Completed sections should feel satisfying without fireworks.

TECHNICAL APPROACH
- Create a Voronoi-like partition of the uploaded image into irregular cells.
- Each tile should be draggable independently.
- Each tile should know its home position and current rotation.
- Home slots should remain in the board.
- Board area should remain visually elegant even when tiles are missing.
- Use clipping/masking so each piece displays its own section of the source image.
- Desaturated loose-tile rendering and full-colour placed rendering should be supported cleanly.
- It is okay to fake Voronoi pieces with precomputed polygons or a simplified irregular partition if that makes the prototype more reliable.

MVP SUCCESS CRITERIA
The prototype is successful if:
1. It immediately feels calm and visually distinctive.
2. The upload flow works smoothly and makes it easy to test with a Skybox AI scene.
3. Dragging and snapping pieces feels satisfying.
4. The grayscale-to-colour restoration feels rewarding.
5. The fog reveal structure makes the puzzle feel inviting rather than cluttered.
6. The irregular fractured-tile look feels elegant and readable on mobile.
7. The settings panel makes it easy to tune the experience.

PLEASE DELIVER
- A single self-contained HTML file if possible.
- Clean, commented code.
- Sensible structure so the prototype can be expanded later.
- Make the prototype actually pleasant to interact with, not just technically functional.

PRIORITIES IN ORDER
1. Feel
2. Readability
3. Smooth upload flow
4. Snap satisfaction
5. Fog reveal elegance
6. Useful settings panel
7. Rotation as a secondary mechanic

If tradeoffs are needed, simplify features rather than compromising the calm premium feel.
